<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_magnetic-dipole" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  	<title>Magnetic Dipoles</title>

		<activity>
      <title>Warm-up Activity</title>
      <p></p>
		
		<p>Shown below are two bar magnets.  Predict how they will interact with one another.  What will happen to their motion?</p> 

		<figure xml:id="bar_magnets">
			<title>Bar Magnets Figure</title>
			<caption>Two bar magnets sit side by side, with their south poles facing each other.</caption>
			<image source="mag/bars.png" width = "50%" ><description>Two bar magnets sit side by side, with their south poles facing each other.</description></image>
		</figure>
		</activity>
		
		
    <corollary xml:id="vid-magnetic-dipole">
	<title>Magnetic Dipole Moment</title>
	
	<statement>
		<p>
			
		</p>

		<video youtube="UVDOts2nYUw"><description>Introducing magnetic dipole moments.</description></video>

	</statement>
  </corollary>

  <subsubsection><title>Key Ideas</title></subsubsection>

	
	<definition>
		<title>Magnetic Dipole Moment</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				
			</p>
			<p>The <term>magnetic dipole moment</term>, symbolized by <m>\vec{\mu}</m>, represents the strength and orientation of objects like permanent magnets.</p>

				<ul>
					<li>
						<p>
							The dipole moment is a physical property of the permanent magnet.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							The dipole moment points from the south to the north pole inside the permanent magnet.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							The magnitude of the dipole moment is related to the strength of the magnet.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							A permanent magnet with a dipole moment will create a magnetic field such as the one shown in the figure below.
						</p>
					</li>
				</ul>
				<figure xml:id="fig_mag-field-lines">
			<title>Magnetic Field Lines Figure</title>
    		<caption><alert>Example:</alert> Magnetic field lines created by a permanent magnet.</caption>
    		<image source="MagneticDipoleLines.jpg" width = "60%" ><description>A bar magnet with a north and south pole. The field lines come out of the north pole and curve out and around to the south pole.</description></image>
				
		</figure>

		</statement>
	</definition>

	<assumption xml:id="a_current-loop">
	<title>Magnetic Moment for a Current Loop</title>
	<statement>
		<p>
			
		</p>
		<p>
			Current loops also behave as if they were permanent magnets!
		</p>
		<p>
			A loop with area <m>A</m> carrying current <m>I</m> may be approximated as having a magnetic dipole moment <me>\mu = IA</me>
		</p>
		<p>
			The direction of the dipole moment is the same as the direction of the magnetic field at the center of the loop, which can be found using a right hand rule: curl your fingers in the direction of the current, and your right thumb will point in the direction of <m>\vec{\mu}</m>.
		</p>
	</statement>
</assumption>
	
	
	<subsubsection>
		<title>Activities</title>
		
		<activity>
      <title>Current Loop and Bar Magnet</title>
	  <statement>
      <p></p>
		<p>The figure below shows a bar magnet and a loop with current that is running clockwise when viewed from above.</p>
		<figure xml:id="loop_magnet0">
			<title>Loop Figure</title>
				<caption>A bar magnet and a current loop.</caption>
				<image source="mag/loop0.png" width = "50%" ><description>A bar magnet sits above a loop of current that is running clockwise when viewed from above. The north pole is closest to the loop of current.</description></image>
			</figure>
		<p>What direction is the net force on the bar magnet?</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Anything with a magnetic moment both creates a magnetic field and experiences magnetic forces!  Modeling the loop as a bar magnet gives the picture below, which suggests a downward force on the bar magnet (the bar magnet's north pole is attracted to the <q>south pole</q> of the current loop).
				</p>
				<figure xml:id="loop_magnet1">
			<title>Loop Figure</title>
				<caption>A bar magnet and a current loop modeled as a bar magnet.</caption>
				<image source="mag/loop1.png" width = "50%" ><description>A bar magnet sits above a loop of current that is running clockwise when viewed from above. The north pole is closest to the loop of current.</description></image>
			</figure>
			</solution>
		</activity>

		<activity xml:id="x_bar-magnet">
      <title>Current Loop and Tilted Bar Magnet</title>
	  <statement>
      <p></p>
		<p>The figure below shows a bar magnet and a loop with current that is running clockwise when viewed from above.</p>
		<figure xml:id="loop_magnet">
			<title>Loop Figure</title>
				<caption>A tilted bar magnet and a current loop.</caption>
				<image source="mag/loop.png" width = "50%" ><description>A tilted bar magnet sits above a loop of current that is running clockwise when viewed from above. The north pole is closest to the loop of current.</description></image>
			</figure>
		<p>Predict what you expect to happen to the bar magnet and the loop as time progresses.</p>
			</statement>
			<hint>
				<p>
					How would you expect the bar magnet to <em>rotate</em>?
				</p>
			</hint>
		</activity>
	</subsubsection>

